(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Why is bitter taste sensation considered a survival mechanism, and who is exceptionally sensitive to it?","choices":["Bitter taste has no survival relevance at all, existing only as a byproduct of overlapping sour and salty receptor activation","Only elderly individuals show heightened bitter sensitivity, since childhood taste buds have not yet fully matured","Because salty substances are usually toxic in high doses; elderly adults show the greatest sensitivity to them","Because many poisons (alkaloids like quinine and strychnine) taste bitter, and babies/young children are exceptionally sensitive to bitter taste"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bitter taste (caused by long-chain nitrogen-containing organic substances/alkaloids like quinine and strychnine) is a survival mechanism since many poisons taste bitter; babies and young children are exceptionally sensitive to bitter taste, and thresholds for bitter compounds (e.g., strychnine at 0.0001 mM) are far lower than for other taste qualities. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What percentage of taste buds are found on circumvallate papillae, and where are they located?","choices":["75%, concentrated mainly along the tongue's anterior tip","25%, located along the flat dorsal surface of the tongue","50%, forming a V shape on the posterior surface of the tongue","10%, scattered across the soft palate and epiglottis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Circumvallate papillae, which form a V shape on the posterior surface of the tongue, contain about 50% of all taste buds; foliate papillae (lateral tongue surfaces) and fungiform papillae (flat tongue surface) each contain about 25%. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the function of filiform papillae, the most numerous papillae type on the tongue?","choices":["They exclusively detect bitter and sour tastes, containing the highest density of taste buds","They give the tongue its texture and are responsible for the sensation of touch, containing no taste buds","They contain the majority of taste buds and mediate the tongue's fine touch sensation as well","They are found only on the posterior tongue, forming the boundary of the circumvallate papillae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Filiform papillae are the most numerous lingual papillae, fine cone-shaped structures covering most of the tongue's dorsum; they give the tongue its texture and are responsible for touch sensation, rather than taste. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the approximate lifespan of an individual taste bud?","choices":["10 hours","10 days","10 years","10 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"The average life of a taste bud is about 10 days, reflecting rapid turnover of these sensory structures; the human tongue has 3,000-10,000 taste buds total. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does the cellular mechanism of bitter taste transduction differ from salty, sweet, sour, and umami transduction?","choices":["Bitter taste causes an internal release of Ca2+, with no external calcium required, unlike the other four taste qualities which depolarize via different mechanisms","Bitter taste does not involve calcium signalling of any kind, relying instead purely upon direct voltage-gated sodium channel activation","Bitter taste requires external calcium entry exactly like sour taste, both relying on the same acid-sensing ion channel","All five taste qualities use an identical G-protein pathway requiring external calcium entry through voltage-gated channels"],"answer":0,"exp":"While salty, sweet, sour, and umami tastes cause depolarization of taste cells via different mechanisms, bitter taste transduction causes an internal release of Ca2+, with no external calcium required — and bitter taste sensation is far more sensitive on a molar basis than other taste sensations. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve transmits taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and which transmits from the posterior one-third?","choices":["Anterior 2/3 via the trigeminal nerve (V); posterior 1/3 via the facial nerve","Both regions are transmitted by the vagus nerve (X)","Anterior 2/3 via the facial nerve (VII); posterior 1/3 via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Anterior 2/3 via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX); posterior 1/3 via the facial nerve (VII)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is transmitted via the facial nerve (CN VII), while taste from the posterior one-third is transmitted via the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX); posterior mouth structures are innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X). All signals converge on the solitary nucleus before relaying to the thalamus and cortex. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides the tongue, where else are taste buds found?","choices":["Only on the hard palate and the inner surface of the cheeks","Only within the nasal cavity and along the eustachian tube","Nowhere else — taste buds exist only on the tongue and lips","On the tonsillar pillars, palate, epiglottis, and proximal esophagus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Extraglossal taste buds are found on the tonsillar pillars, palate, epiglottis, and proximal esophagus, in addition to the three papillae types on the tongue itself. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How much of taste adaptation is accounted for by adaptation of the taste buds themselves?","choices":["About 90%, with only a small remainder attributed to a poorly understood central mechanism","About 100%, since central taste pathways play essentially no role in adaptation at all","Taste adapts fully within about 2 seconds, entirely at the level of the taste bud itself","Only about 50%, with the remainder attributed to central adaptation via an unknown mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Taste sensations adapt rapidly, but adaptation of the taste buds themselves accounts for only about 50% of total adaptation; central adaptation must also occur, though its mechanism is not well understood. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Where is the olfactory membrane located?","choices":["On the tongue only","On the superior part of each nostril","On the inferior part of each nostril","In the middle ear"],"answer":1,"exp":"The olfactory membrane, which contains olfactory cells with cilia bearing odorant-binding protein receptors, is located on the superior part of each nostril. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What second-messenger pathway underlies olfactory signal transduction?","choices":["Odorants are transduced via changes in membrane lipid composition, activating phospholipase C to release IP3 directly","Direct opening of ligand-gated calcium channels, bypassing G-proteins and any cyclic nucleotide second messenger","Binding of odorant to receptor activates a G-protein, which activates adenylyl cyclase, producing cAMP that opens sodium channels causing depolarization","Odorant binding activates a G-protein that inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP and causing hyperpolarization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Binding of a chemical odorant to its receptor on olfactory cilia induces G-protein-mediated activation of adenylyl cyclase, producing cAMP, which opens sodium channels and causes depolarization — the beginning of olfactory signal transduction. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Approximately how many genes code for olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs), and how many actually encode working receptors?","choices":["~1000 genes total; only about 400 encode working ORNs","About 100 genes total; roughly half encode working ORNs","~500 genes total; nearly all 500 encode working ORNs","Exactly 50 genes code for all functioning ORNs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Approximately 1000 different genes code for olfactory receptor neurons, but only about 400 of these genes encode working ORNs; each ORN can bind a variety of odorants with varying affinities, producing unique activation patterns for different odorant profiles. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What three physical/chemical properties must a substance have to be smelled?","choices":["It must be solid and heavy at room temperature, entirely water-insoluble, and completely lipid-insoluble to be reliably perceived by receptors","It must be volatile (to be sniffed into the nostrils), at least slightly water soluble (to penetrate mucus), and at least slightly lipid soluble (to interact with the membrane)","It must be non-volatile and dense, entirely lipid-insoluble, and capable of dissolving completely within the surrounding nasal mucus layer","It must be entirely water-insoluble, completely non-volatile, and able to pass directly through the bony cribriform plate structure itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"To be smelled, a substance must be volatile (so it can be sniffed into the nostrils), at least slightly water soluble (to penetrate the mucus and reach olfactory cells), and at least slightly lipid soluble (to interact with the cell membrane). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which ryanodine receptor isoform is found in cardiac muscle, versus skeletal muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle uses RYR-2; skeletal muscle uses RYR-1","Neither muscle type expresses a ryanodine receptor","Both muscle types use an identical ryanodine receptor isoform","Cardiac muscle uses RYR-1; skeletal muscle uses RYR-2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiac muscle expresses the RYR-2 ryanodine receptor isoform (Ca2+-gated, opened by calcium-activated calcium release), while skeletal muscle expresses RYR-1 (voltage-gated, opened via the DHP receptor). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, Ca2+ release is proportional to what variable, versus in cardiac muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials; skeletal muscle Ca2+ release is proportional to extracellular Ca2+ entry through L-type channels","Both muscle types show SR Ca2+ release that is proportional entirely to the amount of extracellular Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated L-type calcium channels","In skeletal muscle, Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials; in cardiac muscle, it is proportional to the amount of Ca2+ entry through the L-type channel","Neither muscle type shows any proportional relationship between calcium release and either action potential frequency or Ca2+ channel activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, SR Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials (voltage-activated calcium release); in cardiac muscle, SR Ca2+ release is proportional to the amount of Ca2+ entering through the L-type channel (calcium-activated calcium release). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which type of channel opens uniquely in cardiac (but not skeletal) muscle action potentials, producing the plateau phase?","choices":["Fast sodium channels only, which also produce the brief 1-2 ms depolarization typical of skeletal muscle","Only potassium channels, which uniquely produce a hyperpolarizing plateau not seen in skeletal muscle","No channel type differs between the two muscle types, since both rely exclusively on fast sodium channels throughout","Slow calcium-sodium channels (L-type Ca2+ channels), which also produce prolonged depolarization"],"answer":3,"exp":"While both skeletal and cardiac muscle open fast sodium channels during the AP upstroke, only cardiac muscle additionally opens slow calcium-sodium (L-type Ca2+) channels, producing the prolonged depolarization plateau not seen in skeletal muscle. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"If EDV is 120 mL and ESV is 20 mL, what are the stroke volume and ejection fraction?","choices":["SV = 100 mL, EF ≈ 0.8","SV = 140 mL, EF ≈ 1.2","SV = 20 mL, EF ≈ 0.17","SV = 120 mL, EF = 1.0"],"answer":0,"exp":"With EDV = 120 mL and ESV = 20 mL: SV = EDV − ESV = 100 mL, and EF = SV/EDV = 100/120 ≈ 0.8 (80%). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Cardiac glycosides like digoxin and digitalis have what type of inotropic effect, and via what mechanism related to calcium?","choices":["No effect on contractility whatsoever, acting instead solely to slow conduction through the A-V node","They only affect heart rate, slowing SA node discharge without altering contractile force at all","A negative inotropic effect, decreasing contractility by actively lowering intracellular calcium concentration","A positive inotropic effect, increasing contractility, associated with increased intracellular calcium concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitalis) produce a positive inotropic effect, increasing contractility, associated with an increase in intracellular calcium concentration available for cross-bridge cycling. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the typical normal values for systolic and diastolic aortic pressure?","choices":["Systolic 60 mmHg, diastolic 40 mmHg","Systolic 120 mmHg, diastolic 80 mmHg","Systolic and diastolic pressures are always equal","Systolic 80 mmHg, diastolic 120 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Typical normal aortic pressure values are a systolic pressure of about 120 mmHg and a diastolic pressure of about 80 mmHg, as reflected in the aortic pressure curve alongside the incisura (dicrotic notch). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is the sinoatrial (SA) node the natural pacemaker of the heart?","choices":["It has the slowest rate of automatic discharge among cardiac tissues, at 40-60/min, compared to the A-V node (70-80/min) and Purkinje fibers (90-100/min)","It is the only tissue in the heart capable of generating an action potential, since the A-V node and Purkinje fibers lack automaticity entirely","The SA node has no automatic discharge at all, requiring continuous excitatory input from the vagus nerve to trigger each heartbeat","It has the highest (fastest) rate of automatic discharge among cardiac tissues, at 70-80/min, compared to the A-V node (40-60/min) and Purkinje fibers (15-40/min)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The SA node is the pacemaker of the heart because it has the highest (fastest) rate of automatic discharge — normally 70-80/min — compared to the A-V node (40-60/min) and Purkinje fibers (15-40/min), so it triggers the next heartbeat before other tissues can reach their own threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism causes the SA node's resting membrane potential to gradually rise toward threshold?","choices":["The SA node has a stable resting potential with no drift, remaining fixed at -90 mV between each heartbeat","A constant leak of Na+ into the cell, since the membrane sits at -55 to -60 mV where fast Na+ channels are inactivated","A constant leak of Cl- into the cell, since the membrane sits at -90 mV where chloride channels remain persistently open","A constant leak of K+ out of the cell, since the membrane sits at -70 mV where potassium conductance is unusually high"],"answer":1,"exp":"The SA node membrane leaks Na+ continuously, and because its resting potential (-55 to -60 mV) is less negative than in ventricular muscle, fast Na+ channels are inactivated at this voltage — the constant Na+ leak instead gradually raises the membrane potential toward threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What ionic events characterize phase 4 (slow depolarization) and phase 0 (rapid depolarization) of the SA node action potential?","choices":["Both phases involve only fast Na+ channels, exactly as seen in ventricular muscle action potentials","Phase 4: inward Na+ \"funny\" current; phase 0: inward Na+/Ca2+ current via slow (L-type) Ca2+ channels","Phase 0 involves only potassium efflux through delayed rectifier channels, without any depolarizing current","Phase 4: outward K+ current; phase 0: inward Cl- current through ligand-gated chloride channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the SA node, phase 4 (slow depolarization) is driven by an inward Na+ \"funny\" current through special channels, while phase 0 (rapid depolarization/the actual action potential upstroke) is driven by inward Na+/Ca2+ current through slow Na+-Ca2+ (L-type Ca2+) channels — fast Na+ channels are not involved because they're inactivated at the SA node's less-negative resting potential. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What ionic events produce phase 3 (repolarization) of the SA node action potential?","choices":["Inactivation of the slow Na+-Ca2+ channels combined with an outward K+ current","A sudden decrease in K+ conductance combined with sustained inward Na+ current","Opening of fast Na+ channels combined with rapid closure of Ca2+ channels","Continued inward Ca2+ current with progressively increasing K+ conductance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Phase 3 (repolarization) of the SA node action potential results from inactivation of the slow Na+-Ca2+ channels combined with an outward K+ current, returning the membrane potential to about -55 mV. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the function of the internodal pathways, and which one carries impulses specifically to the left atrium?","choices":["The posterior internodal pathway carries impulses to the left atrium, while the anterior band supplies only the SA node region","They exist only in diseased hearts, forming as compensatory conduction routes after myocardial infarction","They only conduct impulses within the ventricles, connecting the A-V bundle branches to the Purkinje network","They transmit the cardiac impulse throughout the atria; the anterior interatrial band carries impulses to the left atrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior, middle, and posterior internodal pathways transmit the cardiac impulse throughout the atria from the SA node; the anterior interatrial band specifically carries impulses to the left atrium. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Where does most of the delay in cardiac impulse conduction occur, and how long is that delay?","choices":["There is no measurable delay anywhere in the conduction system between the SA node and ventricles","In the atrial muscle itself, about 0.08 seconds, with no additional delay elsewhere","In the Purkinje fibers, about 0.03 seconds, with no delay in the A-V node itself","In the A-V node, about 0.09 seconds (with an additional 0.04 seconds in the A-V bundle)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Most of the conduction delay occurs in the A-V node (about 0.09 seconds), with additional delay in the A-V bundle (about 0.04 seconds) — this delay allows the atria to finish contracting and empty into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is \"vasomotor tone,\" and what area of the VMC generates it?","choices":["A complete, maximal state of vasoconstriction produced only transiently during acute hemorrhage or severe pain","A brief, reflexive burst of vasodilation triggered only by activation of the baroreceptor reflex during standing","A partial, ongoing state of vasoconstriction maintained by continuous sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone from the VMC's vasoconstrictor area","A phenomenon produced exclusively by parasympathetic vagal fibers innervating the sinoatrial node and AV node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vasomotor tone is the partial state of contraction maintained in blood vessels by continuous, ongoing sympathetic vasoconstrictor signals transmitted from the vasoconstrictor area of the VMC — this baseline \"sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone\" maintains basal blood pressure. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the neurotransmitter released by vasoconstrictor nerves under VMC control, and how does epinephrine from the adrenal medulla differ in its vascular effects?","choices":["Epinephrine is the sole vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter released from sympathetic nerve terminals, with norepinephrine playing no vascular role","Dopamine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter; norepinephrine from the adrenal medulla causes only vasodilation via beta-1 receptors","Acetylcholine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter for sympathetic nerves; epinephrine constricts vessels solely through beta-1 receptors","Norepinephrine is the vasoconstrictor neurotransmitter; epinephrine can also dilate vessels via a potent beta-2 receptor effect, in addition to constricting via alpha receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Norepinephrine is the neurotransmitter for sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerves; the adrenal medulla secretes both epinephrine and norepinephrine, which constrict vessels via alpha-adrenergic receptors, but epinephrine can also dilate vessels through a potent beta-2 receptor effect. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"How do the roles of the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems differ in circulatory control?","choices":["Both systems act primarily through the same vasoconstrictor fibers innervating arterioles, producing nearly identical effects on resistance","The parasympathetic system controls vascular resistance throughout the body, while the sympathetic system regulates only heart rate","The sympathetic system is important in control of the circulation broadly; the parasympathetic system is mainly important in regulating heart rate via the vagus nerve","The sympathetic system has no role beyond regulating heart rate, while the parasympathetic system controls vascular resistance broadly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sympathetic nervous system is important in control of the circulation generally (including vascular resistance), while the parasympathetic nervous system is mainly important in regulating heart rate via the vagus nerve. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which vessels are NOT innervated by sympathetic nerve fibers?","choices":["Large arteries, large veins, and the coronary sinus","The heart, the large veins, and the pulmonary arterioles","Capillaries, precapillary sphincters, and some meta-arterioles","Small arteries, arterioles, and the postcapillary venules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sympathetic nerve fibers innervate all vessels except capillaries, precapillary sphincters, and some meta-arterioles; innervation of small arteries and arterioles allows sympathetic nerves to increase vascular resistance, and large veins and the heart are also sympathetically innervated. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"In which circulatory beds is the distribution of sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers greatest, and where is it least potent?","choices":["Greatest in the skeletal muscle and lungs; least potent in the kidneys and spleen","Vasoconstrictor fibers are entirely absent from the kidneys, gut, and skin","Distributed equally across all vascular beds, including the coronary and cerebral circulations","Greatest in kidneys, gut, spleen, and skin; less potent in the coronary circulation and brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic vasoconstrictor fiber distribution is greatest in the kidneys, gut, spleen, and skin, but is less potent in the coronary circulation and the brain — protecting these vital organs from excessive constriction during sympathetic activation. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is the normal overall blood flow rate in the circulation of an adult, which is equivalent to cardiac output?","choices":["50 L/min","5 L/min","15 L/min","1 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"The overall flow in the circulation of an adult is about 5 L/min, which is equivalent to the cardiac output — the same value as total blood volume, meaning the entire blood volume circulates roughly once per minute at rest. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the structure of the capillary wall, and what are the two routes for solutes/water to cross it?","choices":["A multilayered stratified epithelium several cells thick; solutes cross only through active, energy-dependent transport pumps","Capillary walls are completely impermeable to all substances, relying entirely on the lymphatic system for tissue exchange","A unicellular layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement membrane; solutes/water cross via intercellular clefts or plasmalemmal vesicles","A thick layer of smooth muscle with no endothelial lining at all, similar in structure to a small arteriole"],"answer":2,"exp":"The capillary wall is composed of a unicellular layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement membrane (capillary diameter 4-9 microns); solutes and water cross via intercellular clefts (spaces between cells) or via plasmalemmal vesicles. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How do lipid-soluble substances (e.g., CO2, O2) cross the capillary wall, compared to lipid-insoluble substances (e.g., water, Na+, glucose)?","choices":["Both cross exclusively via intercellular clefts, since the capillary cell membrane itself is entirely impermeable","Lipid-insoluble substances diffuse directly through the capillary membrane, while lipid-soluble substances require plasmalemmal vesicle transport","Lipid-soluble substances diffuse directly through the capillary cell membrane; lipid-insoluble substances cross via intercellular clefts","Lipid-soluble substances cannot cross the capillary wall at all, requiring active transport via specific membrane carrier proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lipid-soluble substances like CO2 and O2 diffuse directly through the capillary endothelial cell membrane, while lipid-insoluble substances like water, Na+, Cl-, and glucose cross via intercellular clefts (spaces between adjacent endothelial cells). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the approximate width of the intercellular slit pores in capillary walls?","choices":["600-700 nanometers","6-7 nanometers","6-7 micrometers","6-7 millimeters"],"answer":1,"exp":"The width of capillary intercellular slit pores is about 6-7 nanometers, and capillary permeability to different substances varies according to their molecular diameter relative to this pore size. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Based on the relative permeability table, how does albumin's capillary permeability compare to water's?","choices":["Molecular weight has no effect on capillary permeability, which instead depends only on a molecule's electrical charge","Albumin is far less permeable than water (about 0.0001 vs 1.00), due to its much larger molecular weight","Albumin is more permeable than water (about 1.5 vs 1.00), despite its considerably larger molecular weight","Albumin and water have nearly identical permeability, since intercellular clefts admit molecules of any size equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Albumin (molecular weight ~69,000) has a relative permeability of about 0.0001 compared to water's permeability of 1.00, illustrating how capillary permeability decreases dramatically as molecular size increases. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the two major solid structures found in the interstitium, and in what form is most interstitial fluid found?","choices":["Muscle fibers and nerve fibers; most fluid exists as freely mobile liquid rather than a bound gel","Collagen fibers and proteoglycan filaments; most fluid exists as a gel (proteoglycan-fluid mixture) rather than free fluid","Only free-flowing fluid with no solid structures, allowing rapid unrestricted diffusion of all interstitial proteins","Elastin fibers and reticular fibers only; virtually all interstitial fluid exists in a completely free-flowing state"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interstitium contains collagen fibers and proteoglycan filaments (coiled hyaluronic acid molecules) as its major solid structures; almost all interstitial fluid exists as a gel (fluid-proteoglycan mixture), with very little free fluid under normal conditions. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What direction does capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) tend to move fluid?","choices":["It only affects albumin movement, not water or small solutes","Inward, from the interstitium back into the capillary","Outward, through the capillary membrane into the interstitium","It has no measurable effect on interstitial fluid volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) tends to force fluid outward through the capillary membrane, into the interstitial space — one of the four Starling forces governing capillary fluid exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the main stimulus for the increased cardiac output seen during exercise?","choices":["Increased sympathetic activity, along with increased venous return and increased arterial pressure","Parasympathetic stimulation alone, along with decreased venous return and lowered arterial pressure","Decreased sympathetic activity, along with reduced venous return and reduced myocardial contractility","A decrease in venous return caused by pooling of blood within the dilated splanchnic circulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"During exercise, cardiac output increases mainly due to increased sympathetic activity, along with increased venous return (from venoconstriction, increased mean systemic filling pressure, and decreased resistance in exercising muscle) and increased arterial pressure — since a healthy heart pumps out what it receives. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the cardiac index, and what is its approximate normal value for a typical 70 kg adult?","choices":["Total blood volume divided by body surface area, approximately 3 L/m2","Cardiac output divided by heart rate, approximately 100 mL/beat at rest","Cardiac output per square meter of body surface area, approximately 3 L/min/m2","Stroke volume adjusted for body weight, approximately 1 mL/kg per beat"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cardiac index is cardiac output stated per square meter of body surface area; for a 70 kg human with a body surface area of about 1.7 m², the cardiac index is approximately 3 L/min/m2. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does cardiac index change from childhood to old age, and what does this reflect?","choices":["It rises rapidly to over 4 L/min/m2 around age 10 and declines to about 2.4 L/min/m2 by age 80, reflecting declining metabolic activity/muscle mass with age","Cardiac index has no relationship to metabolic activity, being determined instead solely by adult body surface area","Cardiac index remains completely constant at about 3 L/min/m2 from infancy right through old age, entirely unaffected by growth or ageing","Cardiac index steadily increases from about 2 L/min/m2 at birth to over 5 L/min/m2 by age 80, tracking rising body mass"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiac index rises rapidly to a level greater than 4 L/min/m2 around age 10 and declines to about 2.4 L/min/m2 by age 80, since cardiac output is regulated throughout life in near-direct proportion to overall metabolic activity — the decline reflects declining activity/muscle mass with age. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of the following would cause a \"hypoeffective\" heart (decreased pumping ability)?","choices":["Decreased arterial pressure combined with increased vagal excitation of the sinoatrial node","Increased sympathetic activity always produces a hypoeffective heart, regardless of the coronary status","Increased arterial pressure (severe hypertension), coronary artery blockage, valvular heart disease, and cardiac hypoxia","Increased venous return alone, without any change in coronary perfusion or valve function"],"answer":2,"exp":"Factors causing a hypoeffective heart include increased arterial pressure (e.g., severe hypertension) against which the heart must pump, inhibition of nervous excitation, abnormal heart rhythm, coronary artery blockage (heart attack), valvular or congenital heart disease, myocarditis, and cardiac hypoxia. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does strong sympathetic stimulation affect mean circulatory filling pressure?","choices":["It only affects the filling pressure within the pulmonary circulation, leaving the systemic vessels and their capacity unchanged","It decreases mean circulatory filling pressure by dilating veins and thereby increasing overall vascular capacity","It increases mean circulatory filling pressure by constricting systemic blood vessels, decreasing the system's capacity at each blood volume level","It has no effect on mean circulatory filling pressure, since sympathetic tone only alters arteriolar resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Strong sympathetic stimulation constricts systemic blood vessels, decreasing the capacity of the vascular system so that, at each level of blood volume, the mean circulatory filling pressure is increased. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does an increase in mean systemic filling pressure (Psf) affect the venous return curve?","choices":["It shifts the venous return curve upward and to the right","It shifts the curve's plateau downward without changing its slope","It steepens the slope of the curve without shifting its plateau","It only affects the curve's slope, never its plateau value"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater the mean systemic filling pressure (Psf) — reflecting how \"tightly\" the circulatory system is filled with blood — the more the venous return curve shifts upward and to the right; conversely, a lower Psf shifts the curve downward and to the left. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the approximate normal resting coronary blood flow, and what fraction of total cardiac output does it represent?","choices":["Coronary flow is not measured as a fraction of cardiac output","About 10 mL/min, a negligible fraction of cardiac output","About 2000 mL/min, roughly 50% of cardiac output","About 225 mL/min, roughly 4-5% of total cardiac output"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal resting coronary blood flow averages about 70 mL/min/100g of heart weight, or about 225 mL/min total, representing roughly 4-5% of total cardiac output. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"During which phase of the cardiac cycle does coronary blood flow decrease, and why?","choices":["During systole, because cardiac muscle contraction obstructs (compresses) the coronary vessels; the heart is mainly perfused during diastole","During diastole, because ventricular relaxation reduces aortic pressure and thus coronary perfusion pressure","During diastole, because closure of the aortic valve temporarily blocks blood flow into the coronary ostia at that time","Coronary flow is constant throughout the cardiac cycle, since the myocardium never compresses its own vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Coronary blood flow decreases during systole because cardiac muscle contraction compresses and obstructs the coronary vessels running through the myocardium; the heart muscle is therefore mainly perfused during diastole. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are the primary regulators of coronary blood flow, and how tightly is flow linked to myocardial oxygen need?","choices":["Only circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II, with coronary flow being inversely proportional to systemic blood pressure","Coronary flow is fixed by vessel diameter alone and is unrelated to myocardial oxygen consumption or metabolic byproducts","Local metabolic factors (O2 being major, plus adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, K+, NO); coronary flow is directly proportional to the myocardium's oxygen need/consumption","Only neural sympathetic control acting through the beta-1 adrenergic receptors, with coronary flow varying inversely with the heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Coronary blood flow is primarily regulated by local metabolic factors — oxygen being the major one, along with adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, potassium, and nitric oxide (usually triggered by O2 changes) — with flow directly proportional to the cardiac muscle's oxygen need/consumption. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of oxygen delivered in the coronary circulation is extracted at rest, and what does this imply about increasing oxygen delivery?","choices":["About 25% is extracted, leaving abundant reserve capacity to raise oxygen delivery through extraction alone","Nearly 100% is extracted, so any rise in demand can only be met by dramatically slowing heart rate","About 70% is extracted, leaving little room to increase oxygen availability other than by increasing coronary blood flow","Only about 40% is extracted, similar to skeletal muscle, leaving substantial room to raise extraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 70% of the oxygen in the coronary circulation is extracted at rest, leaving little to no room to increase oxygen availability by extracting more — meaning increased myocardial oxygen demand must be met primarily by increasing coronary blood flow. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does low oxygen in cardiomyocytes lead to increased coronary blood flow via adenosine?","choices":["Low oxygen decreases ATP degradation, reducing AMP and adenosine formation; the resulting drop in adenosine causes coronary vasoconstriction, further limiting flow to the ischemic tissue","Low oxygen increases ATP degradation to AMP, some of which is further degraded to release adenosine into cardiac tissue fluid, causing vasodilation; adenosine is later reabsorbed for ATP resynthesis","High oxygen increases ATP synthesis from AMP, releasing adenosine into the cardiac tissue fluid, which then causes coronary vasoconstriction rather than dilation","Low oxygen actually decreases the degradation of ATP to AMP, reducing local adenosine release and causing coronary vessels to mildly constrict rather than dilate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low oxygen in cardiomyocytes increases cellular degradation of ATP to AMP; small portions of this are further degraded to release adenosine into the heart's tissue fluid, causing local coronary vasodilation — after which much of the adenosine is reabsorbed into cardiac cells to be reused for ATP production. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do epicardial and intramuscular coronary vessels differ in their adrenergic receptor populations, and what is the usual net effect of sympathetic stimulation?","choices":["Coronary vessels express only beta-2 adrenergic receptors throughout their length, with alpha receptors confined entirely to peripheral skeletal muscle vasculature","Epicardial vessels have more beta receptors while intramuscular arteries have more alpha receptors; sympathetic stimulation of the coronary vasculature usually produces net dilation","Epicardial coronary vessels have a preponderance of alpha receptors, while intramuscular arteries have more beta receptors; sympathetic stimulation usually causes slight overall coronary constriction","Both epicardial and intramuscular coronary vessels have identical, evenly mixed receptor populations, so sympathetic stimulation always produces uniform dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epicardial coronary vessels have a preponderance of alpha receptors while intramuscular arteries have more beta receptors; sympathetic stimulation of coronary vessels can theoretically cause slight dilation or constriction but usually causes slight overall constriction, with some individuals showing disproportionately severe alpha-mediated vasospasm (vasospastic myocardial ischemia/angina) during excess sympathetic drive. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Who developed the Snellen chart used for visual acuity testing, and in what year?","choices":["Thomas Young, in 1801","Hermann von Helmholtz, in 1850","Isaac Newton, in 1700","Herman Snellen, in 1862"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Snellen chart, used to measure visual acuity via the 20/20 notation system, was developed by Herman Snellen in 1862. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does 20/15 vision mean?","choices":["The person has worse than normal vision, needing to stand closer than 20 feet to see what a normal eye resolves at 15 feet","It means the person is legally blind, since acuity below 20/200 in the better eye meets the clinical definition of blindness","The person can see at 20 feet what a person with normal (20/20) vision could only see at 15 feet — better than normal acuity","The measurement is not a valid Snellen notation, since standard Snellen charts only pair 20 feet with denominators of 20 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"20/15 vision means the person can see at 20 feet what a person with standard 20/20 vision could only see at 15 feet — indicating better than normal visual acuity. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"At approximately what rate is aqueous humor produced by the ciliary body?","choices":["2-3 microliters/min (about 3-4 mL/day)","Impossible to quantify with current methods","About 5 mL per day, produced only at night","1 microliter per hour, at most"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aqueous humor is produced by the ciliary body at a rate of about 2-3 microliters per minute, amounting to roughly 3-4 mL per day. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What long-term condition is identified as a risk factor for glaucoma, related to intraocular pressure?","choices":["Long-term (systemic) hypertension, which can increase the rate of aqueous humor production","Long-term hypotension, which reduces ciliary body perfusion and slows aqueous humor turnover","Chronic accommodation of the lens, which gradually stiffens the ciliary muscle and elevates pressure","Excessive vitamin A intake, which thickens the trabecular meshwork and blocks normal outflow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Long-term systemic hypertension is identified as a risk factor for glaucoma, since it can increase the rate of aqueous humor production, and chronically elevated intraocular pressure can damage the optic nerve axons and blood vessels, potentially causing blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why doesn't the presence of neural elements in front of the photoreceptors significantly degrade the retinal image?","choices":["Because neural elements are located entirely behind the photoreceptors, so light reaches rods and cones without obstruction","Because light never actually passes through neural elements in the retina, traveling instead through vascular channels around them","Because only cones are affected by the neural layer, while rods sit in a separate plane entirely unobstructed by overlying cells","Because the neural elements are virtually transparent in vivo, even though light must pass through them to reach the rods and cones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light must pass through the retina's neural elements before reaching the light-sensitive rods and cones, but this doesn't significantly degrade image quality because the neural elements are virtually transparent in vivo. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Besides preventing light scattering, what important substance does the pigment layer of the retina store in high levels?","choices":["Glycogen, stored as an energy reserve for photoreceptors","Glucose, used for rapid ATP production in the dark","Vitamin A, needed for phototransduction","Sodium, maintaining osmotic balance across the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"In addition to containing melanin (which prevents light scattering), the pigment layer of the retina contains high levels of vitamin A, which is needed for phototransduction (regeneration of retinal for rhodopsin/photopsin). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What type of electrical signal do most retinal neurons (other than ganglion cells) use to transmit information, and why is this suited to encoding light intensity?","choices":["Electrotonic (graded) potentials, which allow conduction of signal strength proportional to light intensity","Only chemical neurotransmitter diffusion, bypassing any electrical membrane potential change entirely","Action potentials exclusively, which are all-or-none regardless of light intensity level","No electrical signaling occurs in the retina until signals reach the ganglion cell layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Most retinal neural elements use electrotonic (graded) potentials rather than action potentials to move current along their membranes, allowing graded conduction of signal strength proportional to light intensity — ganglion cells (and some amacrine cells) are the exception, using action potentials. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Do retinal ganglion cells fire only when stimulated by light, or do they have baseline activity?","choices":["They only fire briefly at light onset, remaining completely silent otherwise regardless of stimulus","They never generate action potentials, transmitting information only through graded electrotonic potentials","They are spontaneously active with continuous background action potentials, upon which visual signals are superimposed","They fire only in complete darkness, becoming entirely silent as soon as light intensity increases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Retinal ganglion cells are spontaneously active with continuous background action potentials; visual signals are superimposed on this background activity, and many ganglion cells are excited by changes in light intensity, responding especially to contrast borders. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What retinal cell type mediates lateral inhibition, and what effect does this have on visual contrast?","choices":["Bipolar cells alone provide lateral excitatory feedback that spreads light signals broadly across the retina, actively reducing overall visual contrast","Amacrine cells alone mediate the lateral spread of excitation, allowing signals to blend broadly together and reducing the sharpness of contrast borders","Ganglion cells provide excitatory feedback to the nearby bipolar cells which amplifies the lateral spread of light, substantially decreasing overall edge sharpness in the image","Horizontal cells provide inhibitory feedback to rods, cones, and bipolar cells, preventing lateral spread of light excitation and enhancing visual contrast (excitatory center, inhibitory surround)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Horizontal cells provide inhibitory feedback (always inhibitory) to rods, cones, and bipolar cells, preventing lateral spread of light excitation across the retina — this creates an excitatory center/inhibitory surround pattern that enhances visual contrast. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the optic disc, and why does it create a blind spot?","choices":["The point where the suspensory ligaments attach the lens to the ciliary body around its full circumference","A structure with no real functional significance, containing a dense population of both rods and cones","The point where ganglion cell axons exit the eye to form the optic nerve; it creates a blind spot because there are no rods or cones there","The very centre of the fovea, which contains only cones and produces the single sharpest area of visual acuity anywhere in the retina"],"answer":2,"exp":"The optic disc (optic nerve head) is where approximately 1 million ganglion cell axons exit the eye to form the optic nerve (and where retinal blood vessels enter); because there are no rods or cones at this location, it creates a natural blind spot in each eye. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is a common functional role of amacrine cells in retinal processing?","choices":["They are interneurons (about 30 different types) whose primary targets are ganglion cells; some respond to signal onset, some to signal extinguishment, and some to movement of light across the retina","They only transmit their signals directly onward to the optic nerve fibres, entirely bypassing both the bipolar and ganglion cells without carrying out any meaningful signal processing","They have no role in visual signal processing at all, functioning solely as structural support cells within the outer nuclear layer of the retina","They exclusively detect color information, with about 3 distinct subtypes each tuned to a single cone photopigment and no other retinal function at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Amacrine cells are a type of interneuron (about 30 different types) whose primary targets are ganglion cells; different amacrine cell subtypes respond to the onset of a visual signal, the extinguishment of a signal, or the movement of a light signal across the retina, contributing to early visual signal analysis. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How do P (parvocellular) and M (magnocellular) ganglion cells differ functionally?","choices":["Both the P and M cell types are functionally identical, projecting to the very same magnocellular LGN layers with exactly matched receptive field sizes and conduction speeds","P cells have small receptive fields, slow conduction, and are sensitive to color/fine detail; M cells have larger receptive fields, fast conduction, and are more sensitive to low-contrast black-and-white stimuli","Only M cells project to the lateral geniculate nucleus at all, while P cells instead project exclusively to the superior colliculus for reflexive eye movements","P cells have large receptive fields and fast conduction with complete color insensitivity; M cells have small receptive fields and detect fine color detail"],"answer":1,"exp":"P (parvocellular) ganglion cells have small receptive fields, slow impulse conduction, and are sensitive to color and fine detail (relatively insensitive to low-contrast signals), projecting to parvocellular LGN layers; M (magnocellular) ganglion cells have larger receptive fields, fast conduction, and are more sensitive to low-contrast black-and-white stimuli, projecting to magnocellular LGN layers. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 32"}]};
